//? Title: Test multivariate normality with Mardia and Henze–Zirkler
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare three diagnostics for Gaussianity

Twelve synthetic observations each have three measurements. The final
observation is displaced along several coordinates. Mardia's tests
measure multivariate skewness and kurtosis separately. Henze–Zirkler
compares whitened observations to a Gaussian reference using pairwise
distances.

```{cpp, id=multivariate-normality, file="figures/multivariate_normality_tests.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/multivariate_normality.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    linalg::DenseMatrix<double> measurements(12,3);
    for (std::size_t row = 0; row < measurements.rows(); ++row) {
        const double i = row+1;
        measurements(row,0) = std::sin(.3*i)+.2*std::cos(2*i);
        measurements(row,1) = std::cos(.4*i)+.15*std::sin(i);
        measurements(row,2) = std::sin(.9*i)+.12*i;
    }
    measurements(11,0) += 2;
    measurements(11,1) -= 1;
    measurements(11,2) += 3;
    const auto mardia = stats::mardia_test(measurements);
    const auto hz = stats::henze_zirkler_test(measurements);
    std::println("| Test | Reference statistic | p-value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| Mardia skewness | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",mardia.skewness_chi_squared,mardia.skewness_p_value);
    std::println("| Mardia kurtosis | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",mardia.kurtosis_z,mardia.kurtosis_p_value);
    std::println("| Henze–Zirkler | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",hz.statistic,hz.p_value);

    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto plot = graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.bars({1,2,3},{mardia.skewness_p_value,mardia.kurtosis_p_value,hz.p_value},
              "",graphics::RGBA{30,86,147},.56)
        .abline_h(.05,graphics::RGBA{166,73,33},1.8)
        .title("Multivariate normality tests").x_label("").y_label("Approximate p-value")
        .x_ticks({1,2,3},{"Mardia skewness","Mardia kurtosis","Henze–Zirkler"})
        .y_limits(0,.6).size(720,420).save_svg("build/multivariate_normality_tests.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Test | Reference statistic | p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Mardia skewness | 15.837 | 0.104 |
| Mardia kurtosis | -0.663 | 0.507 |
| Henze–Zirkler | 0.853 | 0.019 |
\image(figures/multivariate_normality_tests.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The orange line marks p = 0.05 for each individual test.)
\alttext(The bars show p-values of 0.104 for Mardia skewness, 0.507 for Mardia kurtosis, and 0.019 for Henze–Zirkler. Only the Henze–Zirkler bar falls below the reference line.)

Henze–Zirkler yields the smallest p-value in this example. The two
Mardia tests target individual moments, while Henze–Zirkler can respond
to other departures from Gaussianity. All three reference probabilities
are approximate.

[Mardia (1970)|https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/57.3.519] · [Henze and Zirkler (1990)|https://doi.org/10.1080/03610929008830400]
